@node Weather
@chapter Weather

The weather screen shows a forecast for each configured city: the
current conditions, a temperature curve with precipitation for the next
24 hours, and a table of the coming days.

@section Data source

Forecasts come from the Norwegian Meteorological Institute (MET Norway),
through the @samp{complete} product of its Locationforecast 2.0 API at
@url{https://api.met.no/weatherapi/locationforecast/2.0/}.  It is based
on the ECMWF global model, refined with MET Norway's high-resolution
Nordic model (MEPS) where that model reaches, and post-processed with
observations.
Unlike the @samp{compact} product it also carries the probability of
precipitation, gusts and uncertainty percentiles, which the screen uses.

The data is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
license (CC BY 4.0); credit: @emph{Data from MET Norway}.

MET Norway's terms of service (@url{https://api.met.no/doc/TermsOfService})
ask every client to identify itself in the @code{User-Agent} header,
to send no more than four decimals of latitude and longitude, to cache
responses, never to ask again before the @code{Expires} time of the
previous answer, and to use @code{If-Modified-Since}.  raspdash does
all of this; set @code{user_agent} in @code{[app]} to something that
identifies you if you run many dashboards.

@section Screen

Each city gets a box titled with its name and its local time.

@itemize @bullet
@item
The first lines show the current weather symbol and description, the
temperature and how it feels, the wind (an arrow pointing where the
wind blows to, the compass direction it comes from, its speed and
gusts), relative humidity, sea-level pressure, cloud cover, and the
precipitation expected in the next hour with its probability.  Items
that do not fit the box are left out from the right.

@item
The chart shows the temperature over the next 24 hours as a line of
Braille dots, the highest and lowest values on the left, a bar for
every hour with precipitation (scaled to the label under the lowest
temperature, at least 1@tie{}mm), and the local hour every three or
six hours.

@item
The table lists today and the following days: the symbol and
description most typical of the daytime, the lowest and highest
temperature, total precipitation and the highest probability of
precipitation.  Days begin at local midnight of the city.
@end itemize

With four cities and a wide enough terminal the boxes form a two by two
grid; on a terminal narrower than 90 columns they are stacked.  When a
box is short, the chart is dropped first so that at least five days
remain in the table.

When @code{[app] icons} is @samp{nerd}, weather symbols, wind arrows
and the humidity, pressure and cloud markers use the Weather Icons
glyphs of a Nerd Font; with @samp{unicode} they fall back to symbols
such as @samp{☀}, @samp{☁}, @samp{☂}, @samp{❄} and @samp{↯}.

If a city cannot be fetched, its box keeps the last forecast and shows
the error on its bottom line; the footer reports how many cities
failed.

@section Configuration

@example
[weather]
interval = 7200
days = 7

[[weather.cities]]
name = "Saint Petersburg"
lat = 59.9386
lon = 30.3141
altitude = 11
timezone = "Europe/Moscow"
@end example

@table @code
@item interval
Seconds between checks for a new forecast; default 7200 (two hours).
MET Norway updates its forecasts about once an hour.  A check never
happens before the @code{Expires} time of the previous response, and
an unchanged forecast is answered with a short ``304 Not Modified''.
Pressing @kbd{r} checks at once, within the same rule.

@item days
Rows in the daily table, at most; default 7.  The forecast reaches about
nine days ahead.

@item api
The Locationforecast URL; default
@samp{https://api.met.no/weatherapi/locationforecast/2.0/complete}.

@item cities
An array of tables, one per city, shown in order.  There are no
built-in places: without any, the screen asks for them.

@table @code
@item name
Shown in the box title.
@item lat
@itemx lon
Position in decimal degrees, north and east positive.  Only four
decimals (about 10@tie{}m) are sent.
@item altitude
Height above sea level in metres, optional.  MET Norway corrects the
temperature for it; without it, MET Norway uses its own terrain model,
which can be off by tens of metres in hilly places.
@item timezone
IANA time zone name such as @samp{Europe/Athens}, used for the local
time in the title, the hour labels and the day boundaries.  Defaults
to the system time zone.
@end table
@end table

@section Adding a city

Look the place up, for example with
@url{https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org} or the Open-Meteo geocoding
API, which also report elevation and time zone:

@example
curl 'https://geocoding-api.open-meteo.com/v1/search?name=Helsinki'
@end example

Then add a @code{[[weather.cities]]} table with its name, @code{lat},
@code{lon}, @code{altitude} and @code{timezone}, and restart raspdash.

@section Internals

The code lives in @file{src/weather.cpp}.

@table @asis
@item Parsing
@code{weather::parse_forecast} reads the GeoJSON into a list of
@code{Point}s, one per time step.  Each holds the instant values at its
time and, when present, the summaries for the next one and next six
hours.  Values the API leaves out are NaN (test with
@code{weather::has}), missing symbols are empty strings.  The time
series is hourly for roughly the first two and a half days and
six-hourly after that.

@item Daily summaries
@code{weather::daily} gives each point the time until the next one: an
hourly point contributes its one-hour precipitation, probability and
symbol, a six-hourly point its six-hour values.  Every value is then
spread over the hours it covers, so a six-hour block that crosses local
midnight puts half of its precipitation on each day.  Instant
temperatures count for the day they fall on; a six-hour block's minimum
and maximum count for the day holding the block's midpoint.  A symbol
weighs 1 for every hour between 08:00 and 20:00 local time and 0.01 at
night, and the heaviest wins, so nights decide only when nothing else
does.  Days after the first that the forecast covers for less than six
hours are dropped.

@item Symbols
@code{weather::symbol} strips the @samp{_day}, @samp{_night} and
@samp{_polartwilight} suffixes, handles the five plain codes, and
composes the rest from an optional @samp{light}/@samp{heavy} prefix,
@samp{rain}, @samp{sleet} or @samp{snow}, an optional @samp{showers}
and an optional @samp{andthunder}.  MET Norway spells two codes
@samp{lightssleetshowersandthunder} and
@samp{lightssnowshowersandthunder}; the stray @samp{s} is accepted.
Unknown codes show as @samp{?} with the raw code.  Night variants pick
the night Nerd Font glyphs for clear, fair and partly cloudy skies.

@item Fetching and caching
@code{weather::MetSource} is a single source for all cities.  On each
run it asks for every city whose previous response has expired,
sending @code{If-Modified-Since} with the stored @code{Last-Modified};
a 304 keeps the parsed forecast.  @code{weather::HttpCache} holds the
two headers per city.  Truncation to four decimals goes through
@code{weather::truncate_coordinate}, which nudges the value by
@math{10^{-7}} first so that a coordinate such as 25.4626, stored in
binary as 25.462599@dots{}, is not cut to 25.4625.

Each response body is saved in the disk cache under a key made of the
truncated coordinates (@file{weather-59.9386_30.3141.json}), so editing
a city in the configuration never shows another place's forecast.  A
sidecar @file{@dots{}.json.meta} holds the @code{Last-Modified} and
@code{Expires} lines.  After a 304 the body is saved again so that the
file's modification time records when the data was last confirmed.

At start-up @code{MetSource::restore} parses every cached city and
reports the oldest save time, so the screen fills at once and no
request is made while the data is younger than @code{interval}.  If
any city is missing from the cache it reports nothing, which makes the
poller fetch at once; cities that were restored then cost only a
conditional request, or none while their @code{Expires} lies ahead.

Failures are recorded per city.  The run as a whole fails (and the
footer shows it, with the poller's back-off) when any city failed,
naming the first one; the other cities keep their data.

@item Layout
Boxes are laid out two by two when the screen area is at least 90
columns wide.  Inside a box the three ``now'' lines come first; the
chart is drawn only if, after it and a blank line, at least five table
rows remain, and it takes at most ten rows (two of them for the
precipitation bars and the hour labels).
@end table
